# `const r = readings[i]; sum = sum + r * r`: the element is held by a name and the product takes it twice, so it has no one user. Its first user is the product: the loop reckons with the
# elements, and the list, of which nothing is known, is asked in front of the loop what it holds, as for `readings[i] * readings[i]`. In the copy for numbers the turn reads, multiplies and adds.
